\f1\fs22 The knock at the door was timid\'97uncharacteristic, Sarah thought, for anyone who lived on Eloise Shakti\'92s compound. She opened the door cautiously, already expecting trouble, and found herself greeted by the distinctly awkward smile of Johnny Stoat.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah, my dear! Looking radiant as ever,\'94 Johnny exclaimed with the enthusiasm of a door-to-door salesman and a quota to meet. He teetered in place, holding out something in one hand that appeared to be a gift bag, and in the other, what looked suspiciously like an orchid encased in cellophane.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Johnny,\'94 Sarah said flatly, already exhausted. \'93What a surprise.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I thought I\'92d come to check on you. You know, after the... stress of recent events.\'94 He stepped past her into the house without waiting for an invitation, a manoeuvre executed with the smug nonchalance of someone convinced his presence could only be delightful.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shifted to block the opening toward the lounge.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny took this as the general spirit of hospitality and bounded up the stairs instead.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shut the door, took a deep breath, and turned in time to see him at the top landing, peering down the hallway like her bedroom might be where people tossed away unwanted boundaries.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You mean the whole elevator assassination attempt thing?\'94 she called up, already tired of the conversation she was having. \'93Or the part where the Elvi informally canonised me as 
\f2\i Holy Momma
\f1\i0 ?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Both valid causes for concern.\'94 Johnny nodded sagely, his eyes flitting briefly to her belly before quickly retreating skyward. \'93You\'92ve been through a lot. Women in your condition\'97uh, your unique condition, I mean\'97need to rest!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He had reached her bedroom door. He paused there, listening to nothing in particular, as if silence might have an agenda.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Johnny,\'94 Sarah called, pleasant as a polished knife. \'93Downstairs. Now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny reappeared with the same eager smile, slightly out of breath, as if he\'92d just completed a meaningful task.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah arched an eyebrow. \'93Rest? You do know I work for Eloise Shakti, right? That\'92s not really part of the benefits package.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny let out a small laugh, clearly anticipating that his follow-up gesture would smooth over all concerns. \'93Well, you\'92ll be pleased to know I\'92ve come prepared.\'94 He thrust the gift bag toward her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah took it and pulled out its contents. \'93Stress balls?\'94 she asked incredulously, holding up two squishy spheres emblazoned with corporate motivational slogans. She read \'93\'91Crunch Those Deadlines!\'92 and, \'91Stress Is For Losers.\'92 Seriously, Johnny?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And there\'92s more!\'94 Johnny piped up, visibly flustered but doubling down. He gestured to the orchid. \'93See? Something lovely and serene to brighten the place. It calms the mind, or so the lady at the garden centre assured me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I could really use something that calms you, Johnny,\'94 she muttered under her breath. \'93Like a solid dose of morphine.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nothing says concerned coworker like an executive stress ball, right?\'94 he went on, oblivious to the razor edge creeping into her demeanour. \'93Plus, this isn\'92t just a social call. I wanted to share some ideas about your involvement in the Gyges project. You\'92ve been such a tremendous asset, but I worry\'97ahem\'97that perhaps it\'92s taking a toll. Your health comes first, of course.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah froze for a moment, her tempered smile sharpening slightly. \'93Oh? And what did you have in mind exactly?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well\'85\'94 Johnny hesitated, already teetering too far forward to retreat. \'93I think it would do wonders if you delegated more responsibilities. Take a step back. Trust us to carry the heavier burdens of the research for a while! You could focus on\'97\'94 He gestured vaguely to her abdomen. \'93You know.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Let me get this straight,\'94 Sarah said, crossing her arms and tilting her head just enough to imply danger. \'93You\'92d like me\'97who Eloise herself put in charge of overseeing the project\'97to gracefully vanish so you can\'85 what, Johnny? Take the lead?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not take the lead. No, no, no. More like\'85\'94 He seemed unable to verb his way out of trouble, his hands fluttering as though searching for an alibi. \'93Support from a position of\'85 um\'85 visionary oversight?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah forced a tired smile and leaned forward slightly. \'93Visionary oversight? Is there any kind of sight that does not involve vision?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That made him backpedal, both verbally and physically. \'93Sarah, what I mean is\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Let me save you the trouble.\'94 Sarah dropped the bag on the marble-topped console in the foyer from a height that yielded a signature paper-bag splat so familiar it might as well have been trademarked. \'93I\'92m fine, Johnny. And I don\'92t intend to step back. Not from Gyges, not from anything.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of course! Of course \'85 not.\'94 Johnny sputtered, retreating toward the door like a defeated contestant on a reality show. \'93I just\'85 all I\'92m saying is, if you ever need someone to lighten the load, you can count on me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah followed him to the door, her hands clasped thoughtfully in front of her. Something in her demeanour softened, catching Johnny off guard. \'93You know what, Johnny? I think you\'92re right.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh!\'94 His face lit up. \'93You do?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes. You\'92ve convinced me. I\'92ll take tonight off. Total rest while you fully step up and finalise the Phase Four configuration parameters. And, of course, you\'92ll draft Eloise a thorough report while you\'92re at it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny blinked. \'93Phase Four... configuration?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah opened the door wide and ushered him out with a smile sweeter than poisoned honey. \'93Thank you, Johnny. You\'92re such a team player.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>By the time Johnny realised he\'92d just been assigned the most tedious and ambiguous task on the docket, he was halfway down the garden path. He turned back to protest, but Sarah had already shut the door.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, she leaned against it and laughed softly to herself. She felt like a chess master playing someone who thought they held all the aces.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With a pang of regret, she realised that alongside Johnny and Alexander and\'97come to think of it\'97most other men, who courted as if she was a prize-winning porker looking for a man\'92s pen to roll around in, Tony was the one true knight in somewhat tarnished armour.
